From e0a844c0dde12b38629aaeadfbb78fc106aa0873 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: 0xalpharush <0xalpharush@protonmail.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 12:05:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] [compiler-rt] sealed init: record __msan_init's frame
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

🤖: The sealed static runtime describes the main thread's stack from a frame it
records on the way in, instead of reading /proc/self/maps for it.  __asan_init
records that frame in AsanInitInternal, right after SanitizerToolName;
__msan_init never did, so in a sealed MSan link sealed_init_frame stayed 0.

What that costs: GetThreadStackTopAndBottom reads sealed_init_frame as the top
of the sealed main-thread stack window; with it 0 the window top falls back to
the frame of the reader, which is below SetThreadStackAndTls's frame, and
CHECK(AddrIsInStack(&local)) in msan_thread.cpp fails on the runs where the
reader's frame lands low enough.

Taken at the top of __msan_init, above every frame that will be compared
against it, with a local whose address is the bound.  Mirrors __asan_init; a
store of a constant in an unsealed run, so an ordinary MSan build is unchanged.

sanitizer_sealed.h's comments still say that __asan_init is what records the
frame.  They are left stale on purpose: sanitizer_sealed.h is a shared header,
and even a comment edit there moves line numbers in the .debug_line of every
ASan object that includes it, which changes libclang_rt.asan.a and the md5 of
it that README.md records.  This series changes no shared source.

Measured, x86_64, Linux 6.8, ASLR on (vm.mmap_rnd_bits=28), the sealed clean and
uninitialized-read programs of the Dockerfile's MSan check, 80 runs each.  With
this series but the SealedSetInitFrame call removed: 6 of 80 and 6 of 80 runs
died on

  MemorySanitizer: CHECK failed: msan_thread.cpp:26 "((AddrIsInStack((uptr)&local))) != (0)"

With the call: 0 of 80 and 0 of 80, and 0 of 40 and 0 of 40 again with
vm.mmap_rnd_bits=32.
---
 compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp | 14 ++++++++++++++
 1 file changed, 14 insertions(+)

diff --git a/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp b/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp
index 9a3ead077..161a8d708 100644
--- a/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp
+++ b/compiler-rt/lib/msan/msan.cpp
@@ -25,6 +25,7 @@
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_interface_internal.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_libc.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_procmaps.h"
+#include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_sealed.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_stackdepot.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_stacktrace.h"
 #include "sanitizer_common/sanitizer_symbolizer.h"
@@ -473,6 +474,19 @@ void __msan_init() {
   __asm__ __volatile__("" : : "r"(&__msan_wrap_list_present));
 #endif
 
+  // Record the frame __msan_init started on, for sealed init, exactly as
+  // AsanInitInternal does right after it sets SanitizerToolName.  Sealed mode
+  // describes the main thread's stack from a recorded frame rather than reading
+  // /proc/self/maps for it (see sanitizer_sealed.h, whose comments still name
+  // only __asan_init as the recorder): the window ends at this frame.  Its top
+  // is what MsanThread::Init checks the stack against -- without this,
+  // sealed_init_frame stays 0, the window top falls back to the frame of
+  // whoever reads it, and CHECK(AddrIsInStack(&local)) in msan_thread.cpp fails
+  // on the runs where that frame sits below the real one.  Taken here, above
+  // every frame compared against it; a store of a constant in an unsealed run.
+  uptr msan_init_frame;
+  SealedSetInitFrame(reinterpret_cast<uptr>(&msan_init_frame));
+
   AvoidCVE_2016_2143();
 
   CacheBinaryName();
